epr-meta-version: 1
id: application-lifecycle-composition
purpose: >
  Application UX and framework bindings compose the shared SDK, storage, publication
  and serving contracts. This manifest routes reusable lifecycle work to its producer;
  it does not replace domain manifests or claim implemented working-version support.
---

# app — compose shared application capabilities

Use the [root lifecycle map](../.epr-meta/manifest.md#application-lifecycle--authoritative-homes-and-composition)
and [SDK authority](../elohim/sdk/.epr-meta) before planning persistence, collaboration
or publication. Apps own domain actions and UX; domain app manifests declare their
choices. Shared client libraries bind SDK capabilities to their framework. Author
common draft persistence, offline recovery and lifecycle semantics in the SDK;
authorized peer sync in storage; publication authority in DNA; peer serving in the
hub; web2 accommodation in doorway. Existing misplaced code is migration work,
not precedent for a second implementation in another app.

A sprint names the authoritative producer and its consumer journey. Its proof includes
the applicable offline/resume, authorized-sharing and publication transitions from the
existing working-version standard. Display device-only, copy freshness and publication
states separately. Draft standards are not shipped capabilities: a screen must not
promise private drafts or shared editing before the underlying paths enforce them.
These are review obligations, not an automatic import or dependency gate. More specific
manifests retain their local rules; this manifest does not terminate their cascade.
